<section
  id="problem"
  class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-28"
  aria-labelledby="problem-title"
>
  <h2
    id="problem-title"
    data-reveal
    class="font-display text-4xl leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-5xl"
  >
    Because <code class="font-mono text-[0.8em]">git diff</code> tells you{" "}
    <em class="text-accent">what</em>, not why.
  </h2>
  <div class="text-muted mt-8 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed">
    <p data-reveal>
      Coding agents write code fast. A human still has to understand and approve it, so review is
      now the bottleneck.
    </p>
    <p data-reveal>
      <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">git diff</code> shows what changed. It does not show the
      task the agent was given, why it made a change, whether a block was necessary, or whether the
      agent drifted from scope. Today people paste diffs back into the agent's chat and ask “why did
      you do that?”.
    </p>
    <p data-reveal>
      WHY aims to answer three questions in one place: what changed, how it changed, and why. An
      agent's own explanation can be an after-the-fact rationalisation, so WHY labels every
      explanation by how well it is backed. It never replaces reading the code.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
